<dialog class="modal" id="modal" aria-labelledby="modal-title">
  <h2 class="modal__title" id="modal-title">Rotate the feed token?</h2>
  <form class="modal__body" method="post" action="/ui/shows/<%= encodeURIComponent(it.show.slug) %>/rotate-token"
        hx-post="/ui/shows/<%= encodeURIComponent(it.show.slug) %>/rotate-token"
        hx-target="#modal-root" hx-swap="innerHTML">
    <p>
      This gives “<%= it.show.title %>” a brand new feed URL. Use it if the current URL has leaked —
      the token is what authorises access to this show's feed and audio.
    </p>
    <p><strong>The current URL stops working immediately.</strong> Anyone already subscribed, including you,
      needs to re-add the show using the new URL.</p>
    <%
      /* Being straight about what rotation cannot claw back: already-downloaded
         episodes are on subscribers' devices, and any CDN in front of SelfPod may
         hold cached copies for a while. */
    %>
    <p class="help-note">
      Episodes already downloaded stay on subscribers' devices, and any CDN or proxy in front of SelfPod
      may keep serving cached copies briefly. Rotating stops new access; it cannot un-share what is
      already out there.
    </p>
    <%
      /* Changing the public base URL forwards subscribers automatically, so the
         difference has to be said here rather than left to be discovered. */
    %>
    <p class="help-note">
      There is no forwarding note for a rotated token. Forwarding works by answering at the old address,
      and the point of rotating is that the old address stops answering.
    </p>
    <div class="modal__actions">
      <button class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm" type="button" data-modal-close>Cancel</button>
      <button class="btn btn-danger btn-sm" type="submit">Rotate token</button>
    </div>
  </form>
</dialog>
